    {
      "title": "What will you learn?",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "By the end of this chapter, you should be able to create disk partitions, prepare them for storing files, mount them, make them available after reboot, and manage swap space."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "We will learn the chapter in small pieces, with commands and practical examples."
        }
      ],
      "words": 43
    },
    {
      "title": "1. Understand the basic storage concepts",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Imagine your computer is an office."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Disk: The whole storage cabinet."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Partition: A separate section inside the cabinet."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "File system: The system that organizes files inside a section."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Mount point: The directory through which Linux accesses that file system."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Swap space: Disk space Linux can use to supplement RAM when memory is under pressure."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "easy-peasy.ai"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Example"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Suppose you add a new 10 GB disk to your RHEL server."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "You want to use it to store backup files. The process is:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Find the new disk."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Create a partition on it."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Create a file system on the partition."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Create a directory to use as the mount point."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Mount the partition to that directory."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Configure it to mount automatically after reboot."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Important: Creating a partition does not automatically make it ready to store files. You must also create a file system and mount it."
        }
      ],
      "words": 141
    },
    {
      "title": "2. What is disk partitioning?",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Disk partitioning divides one physical disk into separate logical storage areas."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For example, a disk might contain:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "One partition for the operating system."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "One partition for user data."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "One partition for swap space."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This can help separate data, manage storage, and limit the space used by different purposes."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "MBR and GPT"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "These are two ways to organize a disk's partition information."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Feature"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "MBR"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "GPT"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Full name"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Master Boot Record"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "GUID Partition Table"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Partition limit"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Four primary partitions, with extended/logical partitioning available"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Commonly up to 128 partitions on standard systems"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Disk size limit"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "About 2 TiB"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Much larger"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Modern usage"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Older systems"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Modern systems"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remember: GPT is the modern choice for most new systems. MBR remains relevant for older systems and compatibility needs."
        }
      ],
      "words": 122
    },
    {
      "title": "3. Find and inspect disks",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Before changing storage, identify the correct disk. This is one of the most important administrator habits."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Command 1: lsblk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lsblk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This displays disks, partitions, and their relationships."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Example output:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "NAME   MAJ:MIN RM  SIZE TYPE MOUNTPOINTS"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "vda    252:0    0   20G disk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "├─vda1 252:1    0    1G part /boot"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "└─vda2 252:2    0   19G part /"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "vdb    252:16   0   10G disk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Understand the output:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "vda — the first disk."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "vda1 — the first partition on that disk."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "vda2 — the second partition."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "vdb — a second disk."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "disk — a whole disk."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "part — a partition."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "MOUNTPOINTS — where a file system is currently accessible."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "In this example, /dev/vdb is the new 10 GB disk."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Command 2: Show file system information"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lsblk --fs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This displays file system types, labels, UUIDs, and mount points where available."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "A UUID is a unique identifier for a file system or storage device. It is often safer to use a UUID in the boot-time mount configuration than to rely only on a device name."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Command 3: Display disk partition information"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo parted /dev/vdb print"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This shows the partition table of /dev/vdb."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Here, /dev/vdb is an example. Always replace it with the correct disk in your lab."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Safety warning: Never assume that /dev/vdb is the correct disk on your computer. Partitioning or formatting the wrong disk can destroy important data."
        }
      ],
      "words": 220
    },
    {
      "title": "4. Create a partition using parted",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The parted command lets you create, inspect, and delete disk partitions."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 1: Open the disk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo parted /dev/vdb"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "You enter the interactive parted prompt. This means you can type parted commands directly instead of running separate Linux commands."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 2: Display the partition table"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "(parted) print"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This shows the disk size, partition table type, and existing partitions."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 3: Create a GPT partition table — only on a blank test disk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "(parted) mklabel gpt"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Warning: This replaces the disk's existing partition table. Do not run it on a disk containing data you need."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 4: Create a partition"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "On a blank test disk, create a 2 GB partition starting at 1 MiB:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "(parted) mkpart data xfs 1MiB 2GiB"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The parts mean:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "mkpart — create a partition."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "data — the partition name."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "xfs — the intended file system type."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "1MiB — starting position."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "2GiB — ending position."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The partition will be approximately 2 GiB in size."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 5: Exit"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "(parted) quit"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 6: Verify the partition"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lsblk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "You should see a new partition under /dev/vdb, such as /dev/vdb1."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remember that mkpart creates the partition; it does not create the XFS file system itself."
        }
      ],
      "words": 193
    },
    {
      "title": "5. Create a file system",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "A partition needs a file system before Linux can normally store files on it."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Think of a partition as an empty room. The file system organizes the room so you can place and find things inside it."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Create an XFS file system"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/vdb1"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "mkfs means make a file system."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "xfs is the file system type."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/dev/vdb1 is the partition."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Warning: Formatting can destroy existing data on the target partition. Check the device carefully before running this command."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Verify the file system"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lsblk --fs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Look for xfs in the FSTYPE column."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Other useful commands include:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo blkid /dev/vdb1"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This displays identifying information such as the file system type and UUID."
        }
      ],
      "words": 111
    },
    {
      "title": "6. Mount a file system",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Creating a file system does not automatically make its files accessible through a normal directory. You must mount it."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 1: Create a mount point"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo mkdir /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This creates /data, the directory through which you will access the new storage."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 2: Mount the partition"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo mount /dev/vdb1 /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This attaches the file system on /dev/vdb1 to /data."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 3: Verify the mount"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "findmnt /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Or:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "df -h /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "df -h displays available and used file-system space in readable units such as GB."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 4: Test it"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo touch /data/testfile"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "ls -l /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The first command creates an empty file called testfile. The second lists the directory contents."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Unmount the file system"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo umount /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The command is umount, not unmount."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Unmounting detaches the file system from /data. It does not mean you should delete the directory."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Before unmounting, leave the directory and ensure no process is using it. Otherwise, Linux may report that the target is busy."
        }
      ],
      "words": 159
    },
    {
      "title": "7. Make the mount permanent with /etc/fstab",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "A manually mounted file system may not be mounted automatically after a reboot."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The /etc/fstab file stores information about file systems that should be mounted automatically."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 1: Find the UUID"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo blkid /dev/vdb1"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Example output:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/dev/vdb1: UUID=\"1234-abcd\" TYPE=\"xfs\""
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This UUID is only an example. Use the actual UUID from your system."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 2: Edit /etc/fstab"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vim /etc/fstab"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Add a new line using this structure:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "UUID=1234-abcd  /data  xfs  defaults  0  0"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Each field means:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Field"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Meaning"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "UUID=1234-abcd"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Identifies the file system"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Mount point"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "xfs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "File system type"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "defaults"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Standard mount options"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "0"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Legacy dump backup setting"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "0"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Do not use the traditional boot-time fsck check for this XFS entry"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Use your real UUID, not the example."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 3: Test the configuration"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "After saving the file, run:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo findmnt --verify --verbose"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This checks /etc/fstab for configuration issues. Read any warnings carefully."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "You can then test mounting the configured file systems:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo mount -a"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This attempts to mount file systems configured in /etc/fstab that are not already mounted."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Verify:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "findmnt /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Important: A mistake in /etc/fstab can cause boot problems. Test changes before rebooting."
        }
      ],
      "words": 184
    },
    {
      "title": "8. Understand swap space",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Swap space is disk space Linux can use to hold some inactive memory data when RAM is under pressure."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Imagine your desk is RAM and a storage cabinet is the disk. When the desk becomes crowded, you move items you are not currently using into the cabinet. You can retrieve them later, but it takes longer than keeping them on the desk."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "That is similar to how swap works."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Important points:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "RAM is much faster than disk-based swap."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Swap can help when memory is under pressure."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Swap is not a replacement for having enough RAM."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Swap can be configured using a disk partition or a swap file."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Check the current swap spaces"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "swapon --show"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This displays active swap areas, their sizes, and how much is being used."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "You can also run:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "free -h"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This displays RAM and swap usage."
        }
      ],
      "words": 139
    },
    {
      "title": "9. Create a swap partition",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The RH134 chapter teaches how to create a swap partition and activate it persistently."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The example below follows the book's approach. Use a disposable test disk, not your operating system disk."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 1: Create the partition"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "First, inspect the disk:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo parted /dev/vdb print"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Suppose /dev/vdb already has a partition ending at 1001 MB and sufficient free space follows it. The book demonstrates creating a 500 MB partition:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo parted /dev/vdb mkpart myswap linux-swap 1001MB 1501MB"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This creates a partition named myswap, intended for swap use."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The exact start and end positions must match your disk's actual layout. Never copy these positions blindly."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 2: Find the new partition"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lsblk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Suppose the new partition is /dev/vdb2."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 3: Initialize it as swap"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo mkswap /dev/vdb2"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "mkswap prepares the partition for use as swap space. It writes the required swap information to the device."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Do not run this on a partition containing data you need."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 4: Activate swap immediately"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo swapon /dev/vdb2"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This activates the swap partition without waiting for a reboot."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Verify:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "swapon --show"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 5: Make swap permanent"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Find its UUID:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lsblk --fs /dev/vdb2"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Edit the configuration file:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vim /etc/fstab"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Add an entry like this, replacing the example UUID:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "UUID=your-actual-uuid  swap  swap  defaults  0  0"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Save the file, then test the configuration. You can use:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo findmnt --verify --verbose"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Reload systemd's configuration manager if needed:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo systemctl daemon-reload"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Activate any configured swap spaces that are not already active:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo swapon -a"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Finally:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "swapon --show"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The swap space should appear in the output."
        }
      ],
      "words": 255
    },
    {
      "title": "10. Disable and remove swap",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Sometimes an administrator needs to disable a swap area before maintenance."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Disable a specific swap partition"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo swapoff /dev/vdb2"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This disables the selected swap space. Linux may need to move its contents back into RAM, so the operation can fail if there is insufficient available memory."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Verify:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "swapon --show"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "If you want to stop swap from activating after reboot, also remove or comment out its entry in /etc/fstab."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remember the difference:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "swapoff — disables swap."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "swapon — enables swap."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "mkswap — prepares a device for swap use."
        }
      ],
      "words": 87
    },
    {
      "title": "11. Chapter 6 practical workflow",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Here is the complete process you should understand for the storage lab."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "1. Inspect the disk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lsblk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo parted /dev/vdb print"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Identify the correct disk and its existing partitions."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "2. Create a partition"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Use parted to create the partition on the correct disk."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "3. Prepare the storage"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For a normal file system, use mkfs.xfs. For swap, use mkswap."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "4. Make it usable"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For a normal file system, create a mount point and use mount."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "5. Make it persistent"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Add the correct UUID entry to /etc/fstab, test it, and verify the result."
        }
      ],
      "words": 91
    },
    {
      "title": "12. Common problems and how to troubleshoot them",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Problem 1: The mount command fails"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Check:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lsblk --fs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "findmnt"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Possible causes include an incorrect device name, a missing file system, or an invalid mount point."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Problem 2: The mount disappears after reboot"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Check:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "cat /etc/fstab"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo findmnt --verify --verbose"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The entry may be missing, or its UUID, file system type, or mount point may be incorrect."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Problem 3: Swap does not activate"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Check:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lsblk --fs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo blkid"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "cat /etc/fstab"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "swapon --show"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Confirm that the device was initialized with mkswap, the UUID is correct, and the entry is valid."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Problem 4: The device is busy when unmounting"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo fuser -vm /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This helps identify processes using /data. Close or stop the relevant processes before trying to unmount again."
        }
      ],
      "words": 118
    },
